  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM EST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nShowers and a few isolated storms continue this afternoon across \nthe area. The threat for severe weather is still in place, albeit \nlow. The environment has plenty of shear, it's just a matter of \nwhether we can generate enough instability. There is currently a \nnarrow core of weak instability across northern AL and middle TN. \nThis is where a weak QLCS currently exists. SPC RAP analysis shows \nthis CAPE corridor moving east into our area over the next few \nhours. Current thinking, any appreciable instability will remain \nconfined generally south of I-40. This includes the southern \nCumberland Plateau, southern TN Valley, and southwest NC. In \nthese areas, the threat for an isolated, brief and weak, tornado \nremains in place. The main window of concern will be from 2 PM \nthis afternoon through 8 PM this evening. \n\nElsewhere, a few instance of gusty winds are possible. Any \nheavier shower, or thunderstorm, could bring the higher winds aloft \ndown to the surface. Scattered rain showers continue through the \nnight and linger into Thursday morning. We dry out through the day \nas dry air moves in behind the front. \n\nFriday through the weekend is dry as high pressure will move back in \nfrom the west. Cooler temperatures over the weekend with below \nnormal highs and lows expected. \n\nNBM keeps no POPS through the rest of the extended with temperatures \nreturning closer to normal by early next week.",
